  "diagnosis_text": "`h1 \u2192 h5` is confirmed failed: 3/3 ICMP packets were lost.\n\nThe physical topology is healthy: all five switches are available, the ring links are active, and the relevant host and inter-switch ports on `s1` and `s5` are enabled. This is not a link-down or host-port-down fault.\n\nThe fault localises to an overriding flow entry on **s5**:\n\n- On `s5`, a priority-50000 flow from `org.coms6200.experiment` matches:\n  - source MAC `00:00:00:00:00:01` (`h1`)\n  - destination MAC `00:00:00:00:00:05` (`h5`)\n- Instead of delivering it to `h5` on **port 3**, it outputs it on **port 1**, back toward `s1`.\n\nThat rule overrides the normal baseline rule, which correctly sends frames destined for h5 to port 3.\n\nThere is also a matching high-priority rule on `s1` that sends this traffic out port 1 toward `s2` rather than using the direct `s1\u2013s5` link on port 2. The packet consequently travels around the ring to s5, where the faulty s5 rule sends it back toward s1. The very high counters on these rules and the `s5` port-1 transmit sample (~3,580 packets/s) support a forwarding loop rather than ordinary traffic.\n\n**Diagnosis:** a high-priority, h1-to-h5-specific forwarding rule on **s5** is misdirecting traffic to `s5` port 1 instead of the h5 access port 3, creating a loop with the corresponding policy on s1. The corrective action is to remove or correct that override so matching h1\u2192h5 frames at s5 output to port 3.",
